\section{Discussion}

The STME-RS-A framework introduces a controlled anharmonic deformation of the
Randall--Sundrum geometry, producing a non-equidistant Kaluza--Klein (KK) mass
spectrum with experimentally resolvable signatures. In this section we synthesize
the geometric, phenomenological, and theoretical implications of the model.

\subsection{Geometric Implications}

The quartic bulk term $\lambda_4$ modifies the curvature of the scalar profile
$\Phi(y)$, increasing localization near the IR brane. This deformation shifts the
Sturm--Liouville eigenvalues and produces non-equidistant KK masses. The
anharmonicity is not a perturbative correction but a structural modification of
the geometry.

